==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|lacquey|lacky}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|laquais}}, which is probably (via {{uder|en|pro|lacai}}?) from {{uder|en|es|lacayo}}, itself perhaps from {{uder|en|it|lacchè}} and {{uder|en|el|λακές}}, from {{uder|en|tr|ulak}}. Another possibility is through {{uder|en|fr|-}}, from {{uder|en|ca|alacay}}, from {{uder|en|ar|قَاضٍ|اَلْقَاضِي|magistrate}}. See French {{m|fr|laquais}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlæ.ki/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-lackey.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|æki|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[footman]], a [[livery|liveried]] male servant.
#* {{RQ:Maturin Melmoth|I||198|My dearest father,—I say nothing of them,—but ''I dare'' to speak of myself,—I can never be a monk,—if that is your object—spurn me,—order your '''lacqueys''' to drag me from this carriage,—leave me a beggar in the streets to cry “''fire and water'',”—but do not make me a monk.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Ruskin|chapter=By the Rivers of Waters|title=“Our Fathers Have Told Us.”: Sketches of the History of Christendom for Boys and Girls who have been Held at Its Fonts|volume_plain=part I (The Bible of Amiens)|location=Orpington, Kent|publisher=[[w:George Allen (publisher)|George Allen]]|year=1884|pages=30–31|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/ourfathershaveto00rusk#page/30/mode/2up|oclc=222616845|passage=St. [[w:Martin of Tours|Martin [of Tours]]] looks round, first, deliberately;—becomes aware of a tatterdemalion and thirsty-looking soul of a beggar at his chair side, who has managed to get ''his'' cup filled somehow, also—by a charitable '''lacquey'''. St. Martin turns his back on the Empress, and hobnobs with ''him''!}}
# A [[fawn|fawning]], servile follower.
#: {{syn|en|lickspittle|Thesaurus:loyal follower}}

